Transmission of the pathogen was 78.4% lower in the in the most species-d[iverse] wetland communities, compared with the least species-diverse. Under laboratory conditions, the researchers observed a 64% decrease in pathogen[ transmission and] infection when the number of species in a controlled community was increased from one to three. In a further experiment using an outdoor, artificially assembled community, total infection rates[ decreased] by 50% when the number of species present was increased from one to four.
The study provides evidence of a link between biodiversity and the[ spread] of disease. This is perhaps[ unsur]prising, given that there is plenty of evidence to[ show] that populations with little biodiversity, such as monocultures,[ facilitate] the[ spread] of disease. However, the authors[ also caution] that many other[ environmental] factors,[ including] resource[ availability,] climate and habitat variables will[ interact] to affect the rate of[ spread] of disease within a community.
